  "best_model_id": "PGS002247",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for melanoma are present (multiple models with trait_efo=melanoma and melanoma phenotypes). Among them, PGS002247 is preferred because it targets an endpoint closely aligned to the target trait (phenotyping_reported: incident invasive melanoma) and provides a relatively strong, directly comparable performance summary versus many other melanoma PRSs in the list that either (a) report only full-model AUC without PRS-only metrics, (b) focus on special populations/endpoints (e.g., familial melanoma, second primary melanoma, childhood cancer survivors), or (c) are framework-style UKB pan-trait scores. In the provided metadata, PGS002247 reports full_model_auc=0.691 and an R² estimate (0.072) for European validation (n=4,765), which is more informative than most other direct-match candidates here. Main limitation: the context does not provide PRS-only (no-covariate) AUROC/R² for most models (including PGS002247), making cross-model performance comparisons imperfect and lowering certainty; additionally, some other melanoma models show higher full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS000743/PGS000813) but with smaller or endpoint-restricted evaluations, so a clearly dominant choice is not unambiguous from the visible evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002247",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "There are many direct-match melanoma PRS candidates in the provided context (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743/744/745, PGS000766, PGS000790, PGS001304, PGS002246/002247/002253, PGS003430, PGS003412, PGS004247, PGS004795/004796, PGS004886, PGS005208). Among these, PGS002247 has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: incident invasive melanoma; trait_efo: melanoma) and reports a comparatively strong discrimination metric in the provided fields (full_model AUROC 0.691 with R² 0.072), outperforming other incident-melanoma candidates shown (e.g., PGS002246 AUROC 0.688, R² 0.07; PGS002253 AUROC 0.662, R² 0.051). However, the overall decision is sub-optimal rather than high-quality because PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R²) are not available for these models, so the reported AUROC may be partly covariate-driven (even though covariates here are basic: age, sex), and some other direct-match models show higher full-model AUROC under heavier clinical risk-factor adjustment (e.g., PGS000743 AUROC 0.74 with extensive traditional risk factors), making cross-model comparability unresolved from the visible evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000903",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 11 candidates are direct matches by trait_reported/trait_efo, but several are actually progression/complication endpoints (PGS000056 cognitive decline; PGS000211 motor decline; PGS000777 Parkinson’s disease dementia), which are weaker phenotype alignment for the generic target “parkinson disease”. Among the remaining Parkinson’s disease diagnosis/risk endpoints, PGS000903 (Lancet Neurol 2021 GWAS meta-analysis) has strong, more PRS-informative evidence: large variant count (C+T; 1,805 variants) and the best available discriminative effect estimate (OR 6.25 for top vs bottom quartile) plus reported R²=0.054; it also uses only basic covariates (PCs, age, sex), avoiding heavy clinical packaging. The main limitation is that the reported AUROC appears to be full-model (not explicitly PGS-only) and evaluation is mixed-ancestry (EUR/MAE/SAS) without a clearly isolated PGS-only metric, so certainty over standalone PRS performance remains limited; PGS001774’s higher AUROC=0.8 is discounted because it is also full-model and includes multiple non-genetic risk factors (including family history), making it less comparable as a PRS."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004606","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (macular degeneration / age-related macular degeneration). However, several are framework-style UK Biobank scores (PGS001013 from the “813 traits” sparse framework; PGS001834 and PGS002041 from the “Portability of 245 polygenic scores” framework), which the provided domain guidance flags as generally underperforming disease-focused alternatives when those exist.

Among the disease-focused candidates, PGS004606 is the most strongly supported by visible evidence: it has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: “Age-related macular degeneration”), comes from a disease-focused GWAS meta-analysis context (IAMDGC; Nat Genet 2024), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS) with ~1,000,946 variants, and provides supportive effect-size evidence (OR 1.76 per SD) plus a reported full-model AUROC of 0.71 with only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs). Compared with its sibling PGS004607, PGS004606 has stronger effect size (OR 1.76 vs 1.48) and higher reported AUROC (0.71 vs 0.65) in the same evaluation ancestry.

Main limitation: across most candidates (including PGS004606), PRS-only (no-covariate) AUC/R² is not reported, so the full-model AUROC cannot be treated as a directly comparable PRS metric per the domain guidance; this keeps confidence at Moderate despite strong endpoint/study/method signals."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000471",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates (PGS000469/470/471) are direct matches with consistent endpoint fidelity: trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all specify “Carcinoma in situ of skin” (skin carcinoma in situ), with similar basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4) and the same publication family and EUR-only training/evaluation context. None reports PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R2, so full-model AUROC should be interpreted cautiously; however, within this near-clone set the full-model metrics and effect sizes are the only comparative performance evidence available. PGS000471 shows the strongest reported discrimination and genetic effect size among siblings (full-model AUROC 0.569; Nagelkerke’s pseudo-R² 0.0255; OR per SD 1.401; top 1% vs rest OR 3.77), exceeding PGS000470 (AUROC 0.557; R² 0.0154; OR 1.308) and PGS000469 (AUROC 0.524; R² 0.00141; OR 1.09). Main limitation: because only full-model metrics are provided (no PGS-only AUROC/R2 and no incremental AUC), the degree to which the reported discrimination reflects PRS vs basic covariates cannot be cleanly separated, lowering confidence from High."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002046","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Two direct-match otosclerosis candidates are present (PGS001255, PGS002046). Both align to the target trait via trait_efo=otosclerosis. PGS001255 has an explicit time-to-event phenotype (phenotyping_reported: "TTE otosclerosis"; trait_reported includes "time-to-event"), which is a noted endpoint-fidelity warning signal for generic disease-risk use. It is also from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework paper ("Significant sparse polygenic risk scores across 813 traits in UK Biobank"), which domain guidance flags as tending to generalize poorly. Although PGS001255 reports PRS-only AUROC/R² and incremental AUROC with basic covariates, this evidence is still within the framework context.

PGS002046 uses LDpred2 with a genome-wide variant set (570,308 variants), a structurally stronger method family for polygenic traits than sparse selection, and its phenotype is the generic disease label ("Otosclerosis") without the time-to-event framing. It comes from a portability-focused UKB paper; while that is also a framework-like context, it is not the specifically flagged "813 traits" sparse framework. Performance reporting is limited (partial correlation only; no PRS-only AUROC/R²), which prevents a high-confidence selection.

Given better endpoint framing and stronger method/variant structure but limited comparable metrics, PGS002046 is the best-supported direct-match choice, with moderate confidence and an overall sub-optimal evidence package."}
